  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 215 AM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nThursday Night and Friday...Several solutions continue to \nsuggest that as the LLVL ridge departs off to the east, an \nembedded short wave in steering northwesterly flow aloft will \ncombine with an increasing LLJ with some THTA-e advection to \nproduce an elevated wing of convergent WAA type showers and even\nsome embedded thunder acrs portions of the MO RVR Valley and \ninto central IA Thu night into Friday morning. The models are \nstill varying on the placement and how far north or south this \nwing develops, and how far east it can make it through Friday \nmorning battling lingering ridging acrs the southern GRT LKS/OH \nRVR Valley. The consensus is that some showers and mid deck of \nclouds will get into the local area from the west if even in a \ndecaying fashion. But they also show ongoing convergent LLJ \nprocesses enough to keep some showers festering acrs the area \nfor much of the day Friday until lifting off to the north and \neast Friday evening. The NBM has 15-40% chances for 24-hr QPF of\n0.10\"+ focused north of Highway 34, highest in far west- \ncentral counties.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...Digging long wave upper trof acrs the \nPacific Northwest and ridging amplifying up the GRT LKS will \nresult in warming southwesterlies acrs the MID CONUS and toward \nthe GRT LKS. Saturday will be a full blown warm sector day with \nbreezy southerly winds and increasing thicknesses that support \nhighs in the 80s for much of the area. This may be a day of \nincreased cropland fire danger risk depending on humidity \ntrends. Then eyes turn to the tropical system in the Gulf and \nthe path it may take getting drawn northward inland. A more \nwestern track of the weakening system this weekend could bring a\nlittle light rain into far eastern sections of the outlook \narea. While a slightly more eastward track would result in dry \nconditions for our area. The NBM currently has slight chances \nfor rain on Sunday mainly east of a Princeton to Macomb line.\n\nMonday and Tuesday...A significant drop in temperatures is\npossible early next week as a seasonably strong cold front \nmoves through the Midwest. Based on latest model guidance, the \ndrop in temperatures may hold off until Tuesday or Wednesday. \nBeyond Monday, the NBM is showing a large spread in max temps, \nwith the current mean forecast in the 60s across eastern Iowa \nand northwest Illinois. However, these values could trend lower \nwith 50s possible for highs across portions of the area.",
